Popular science is a well known genre consisting primarily (though perhaps not
exclusively, what with the vagueness of genre boundaries) of works presenting
complex scientific theories, disputes or developments in a way that is more
readily understood by laypeople than the technical scientific publications in
which they are first presented. A price for such accessibility is that
occasionally, at some key point or other, the reader is asked to take some claim
on scientific authority. In seeking to explain one result, the author asks the
reader to accept some other claim for which no justification is given apart form
the fact that it generally accepted by members of the relevant scientific
community.

Of course, other things being equal, the less a
work of popular science relies upon such appeals to scientific authority the
better it is. The better the author is at explaining complex matters the less
they need to rely upon such appeals.

